// =============================================================================
//  sim.hml — THE FIXED-TIMESTEP SIM STEP.  The seam between the two halves.
//                                                                    W4-9.
// =============================================================================
//
//  ARCHITECTURE §4.3 gives ten stages and says the order is API:
//
//      a  apply queued commands (player-slot order, then seq)
//      b  movement integration + terrain/AABB collision
//      c  weapons: fire, reload, cooldowns, recoil_aim
//      d  projectiles
//      e  AI steering
//      f  damage resolution + death
//      g  pickups / interactions / lantern channel / block placement
//      h  day-cycle tick, chunk streaming decisions
//      i  history_capture
//      j  sim_effects  (SKIPPED when world.replaying == 1)
//
//  This file executes exactly that, once per fixed tick, and nothing else in
//  the project may decide when a system runs.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHY THE ORDER IS DATA AND NOT CONTROL FLOW
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  BUILD_PLAN W4-9's acceptance criterion is "the stage order is asserted by a
//  test that reorders two stages and expects a different hash".  A hard-coded
//  sequence of ten calls cannot be reordered by a test without editing this
//  file, which means the assertion would test a COPY of the order rather than
//  the order — the exact failure mode that made Gate 4a's enemy caps mutable
//  with every probe still green.
//
//  So the order lives in `sim.order`, a ten-element `array<i32>` initialised to
//  the identity, and `sim_step` dispatches through it.  The cost is one array
//  read and one integer compare chain per stage per TICK — ten of each, 60
//  times a second, against a 2.0 ms budget.  Measured below the noise floor.
//  `sim_stage_swap` is the test hook, and it COUNTS its use in `sim.swaps` so a
//  shipping build that ever called it is visible in the stats rather than
//  mysterious in the bug report.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHAT `sim_step` TAKES, AND WHY THE WORLD CARRIES THE SIM
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ARCHITECTURE §5.4 writes `sim_step(world, tick)`, and §5.6 writes
//  `rsnap_build(rs, world, alpha)`, and §5.4 writes
//  `snapshot_write(world, viewer_slot, baseline)`.  Three functions, one first
//  argument.  Meanwhile the eight Wave-4a modules each own a state object that
//  has to live SOMEWHERE and must not be a top-level global (a global would
//  make two worlds in one process impossible, which kills both the dedicated
//  server and every A/B determinism test that runs two sims side by side).
//
//  So `sim_new` builds the world through `world_new()` and attaches ONE extra
//  field to it — `world.sim` — holding every module's state.  `world.hml` is
//  untouched, `sim_step(world, tick)` is the documented signature, and the
//  renderer keeps reading the same object it always did.  The alternative
//  (a wrapper object holding the world) would have changed the first argument
//  of three documented functions to buy nothing.
//
//  Reported to ARCHITECTURE §5.4c along with the exact signatures below.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  DETERMINISM — the acceptance criterion
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   * No wall clock.  Not `__clock()`, which is a compiler BUILTIN and needs no
//     import — `ci_imports.sh` R8 exists because of exactly that hole.
//   * No `@stdlib/random`.  Every draw is `rng.hml` seeded from
//     (world_seed, stream, tick, entity).
//   * `TICK_DT` is a compile-time constant.  No function below sees a measured
//     delta; `sim_accum_advance` converts milliseconds into a TICK COUNT and
//     that is the only place a real duration appears.
//   * The accumulator lives here, clock-free, so the sim owns its own pacing
//     rule and `src/game/main.hml` only supplies the measured milliseconds.
//   * Iteration order is world slot order, which is a pure function of the
//     spawn/despawn sequence.  Nothing iterates a hash table.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  THE IMPORT WALL
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  `src/sim/**` may import `@stdlib/math`, other `src/sim/**` and `src/core/**`.
//  This file imports the eight Wave-4a modules plus world/history/command/rng/
//  chunk/worldgen/daycycle/snapshot and `src/core/**`.  No SDL, no wobbleweed,
//  no `src/render/**`, no `src/net/**`, no clock.
// =============================================================================

